#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:3
msgid "The libc code style"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:5
msgid "Naming style"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:7
msgid ""
"For the large part, the libc project follows the general [coding standards "
"of the LLVM project](https://llvm.org/docs/CodingStandards.html). The libc "
"project differs from that standard with respect to the naming style. The "
"differences are as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:12
msgid ""
"**Non-const variables** - This includes function arguments, struct and class "
"data members, non-const globals and local variables. They all use the "
"`snake_case` style."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:15
msgid ""
"**const and constexpr variables** - They use the capitalized `SNAKE_CASE` "
"irrespective of whether they are local or global."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:17
msgid ""
"**Function and methods** - They use the `snake_case` style like the non-"
"const variables."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:19
msgid ""
"**Internal type names** - These are types which are internal to the libc "
"implementation. They use the `CaptilizedCamelCase` style."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:21
msgid ""
"**Public names** - These are the names as prescribed by the standards and "
"will follow the style as prescribed by the standards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:24
msgid "Macro style"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:26
msgid "We define two kinds of macros:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:28
msgid ""
"**Build defined** macros are generated by `CMake` or `Bazel` and are passed "
"down to the compiler with the `-D` command line flag. They start with the "
"`LIBC_COPT_` prefix. They are used to tune the behavior of the libc. They "
"either denote an action or define a constant."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:33
msgid ""
"**Code defined** macros are defined within the `src/__support/macros` "
"folder. They all start with the `LIBC_` prefix."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:36
msgid ""
"`src/__support/macros/properties/` - Build related properties like target "
"architecture or enabled CPU features defined by introspecting compiler "
"defined preprocessor definitions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:40
msgid ""
"`architectures.h` - Target architecture properties. e.g., "
"`LIBC_TARGET_ARCH_IS_ARM`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:42
msgid ""
"`compiler.h` - Host compiler properties. e.g., `LIBC_COMPILER_IS_CLANG`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:44
msgid ""
"`cpu_features.h` - Target cpu feature availability. e.g., "
"`LIBC_TARGET_CPU_HAS_AVX2`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:46
msgid ""
"`types.h` - Type properties and availability. e.g., "
"`LIBC_TYPES_HAS_NATIVE_FLOAT128`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:48
msgid "`os.h` - Target os properties. e.g., `LIBC_TARGET_OS_IS_LINUX`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:51
msgid ""
"`src/__support/macros/config.h` - Important compiler and platform features. "
"Such macros can be used to produce portable code by parameterizing "
"compilation based on the presence or lack of a given feature. e.g., "
"`LIBC_HAS_FEATURE`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:56
msgid ""
"`src/__support/macros/attributes.h` - Attributes for functions, types, and "
"variables. e.g., `LIBC_UNUSED`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:59
msgid ""
"`src/__support/macros/optimization.h` - Portable macros for performance "
"optimization. e.g., `LIBC_LIKELY`, `LIBC_LOOP_NOUNROLL`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:62
msgid "Inline functions and variables defined in header files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:64
msgid ""
"When defining functions and variables inline in header files, we follow "
"certain rules:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:67
msgid ""
"The functions should not be given file-static linkage. There can be class "
"static methods defined inline however."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:70
msgid ""
"Instead of using the `inline` keyword, functions should be tagged with the "
"`LIBC_INLINE` macro and variables should be tagged with the "
"`LIBC_INLINE_VAR` macro defined in `src/__support/macros/attributes.h`. For "
"example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:83
msgid ""
"The `LIBC_INLINE` tag should also be added to functions which have "
"definitions that are implicitly inline. Examples of such functions are class "
"methods (static and non-static) defined inline and `constexpr` functions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:90
msgid "Setting `errno` from runtime code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:92
msgid ""
"Many libc functions set `errno` to indicate an error condition. If LLVM's "
"libc is being used as the only libc, then the `errno` from LLVM's libc is "
"affected. If LLVM's libc is being used in {ref}`overlay_mode`, then the "
"`errno` from the system libc is affected. When a libc function that can "
"potentially affect `errno` is called from a unit test, we do not want the "
"global `errno` (as in, the `errno` of the process thread running the unit "
"test) to be affected. If the global `errno` is affected, then the operation "
"of the unit test infrastructure itself can be affected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:101
msgid ""
"To handle `errno` correctly across all build modes and avoid silent thread-"
"local storage mismatches, the following rules must be followed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:104
msgid ""
"**Header Inclusion:** Runtime code and unit tests must **ONLY** `#include "
"\"src/__support/libc_errno.h\"` and refer to `libc_errno`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:107
msgid "Never `#include <errno.h>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:108
msgid ""
"Never `#include \"src/errno/libc_errno.h\"` (this is a legacy path; the "
"header lives in `src/__support/`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:110
msgid ""
"For standard error constants (such as `EINVAL`, `ENOMEM`), include `\"hdr/"
"errno_macros.h\"`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:113
msgid "Setting `errno` should be done directly via `libc_errno`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:119
msgid ""
"**Where to set `errno`:** `errno` should only be set just before returning "
"from the implementation of the public function. It should not be set from "
"within helper functions. Helper functions should use idiomatic C++ "
"constructs like [cpp::optional](https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/blob/"
"main/libc/src/__support/CPP/optional.h) and [ErrorOr](https://github.com/"
"llvm/llvm-project/blob/main/libc/src/__support/error_or.h) to return error "
"values."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:128
msgid ""
"**CMake Dependencies for Public Entrypoints:** Any entrypoint that reads or "
"sets `errno` should ideally declare **BOTH** of the following in the "
"`DEPENDS` list of its `add_entrypoint_object`:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:131
msgid ""
"**`libc.src.__support.libc_errno`**: Satisfies the header dependency for "
"`src/__support/libc_errno.h`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:133
msgid ""
"**`libc.src.errno.errno`**: Satisfies the link-time requirement for the "
"compiled runtime storage (`libc_errno.cpp`) defining `thread_errno`, "
"`__llvm_libc_errno()`, and the C++ `Errno` class."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:137
msgid ""
"While LLVM-libc generally disallows dependencies between public entrypoints, "
"`libc.src.errno.errno` is an **explicitly allowed exception** in the build "
"system (see `ALLOWED_DEPS` in `libc/cmake/modules/"
"LLVMLibCObjectRules.cmake`). Downstream consumers and test suites that link "
"the entrypoint require this dependency so that the storage for `libc_errno` "
"is linked."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:143
msgid ""
"**CMake Dependencies for Internal Support Libraries:** Header-only utility "
"libraries under `libc/src/__support/` (such as `OSUtil` syscall wrappers or "
"`FPUtil`) that reference `libc_errno.h` should depend on "
"**`libc.src.__support.libc_errno`** so they remain pure header libraries "
"without introducing compiled entrypoint object dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:149
msgid ""
"**Unit and Hermetic Testing:** Unit tests verifying functions that set "
"`errno` must use the `ErrnoCheckingTest` fixture (`test/UnitTest/"
"ErrnoCheckingTest.h`) and `ErrnoSetterMatcher` (`test/UnitTest/"
"ErrnoSetterMatcher.h`)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:153
msgid ""
"Both `ErrnoCheckingTest` and `ErrnoSetterMatcher` already declare `DEPENDS "
"libc.src.errno.errno`, so tests using them inherit the compiled `errno` "
"object transitively."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:155
msgid ""
"Tests that check `libc_errno` directly without those matchers must declare "
"`libc.src.errno.errno` in their test `DEPENDS`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:157
msgid "Tests must never `#include <errno.h>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:159
msgid ""
"**The \"Dual-TLS\" Pitfall in Overlay Mode:** Why is strictly adhering to "
"`libc_errno` and `\"src/__support/libc_errno.h\"` critical? In overlay mode, "
"the host system libc (e.g., glibc) maintains its own thread-local `errno` "
"location (accessed via `*__errno_location()`). In unit tests, LLVM-libc runs "
"in internal thread-local mode (`LIBC_ERRNO_MODE_THREAD_LOCAL`), writing to "
"LLVM-libc's own `thread_errno`. If implementation code or tests mistakenly "
"`#include <errno.h>` and use raw `errno`, the code will compile cleanly "
"without warnings, but reads and writes will target different thread-local "
"storage locations in the same thread. This results in silent test failures "
"or error values that appear to vanish or go to the wrong thread."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:172
msgid "Accessing values from `cpp::expected`, `ErrorOr`, and `cpp::optional`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:174
msgid ""
"Because LLVM-libc builds with `-fno-exceptions`, [`cpp::expected`](https://"
"github.com/llvm/llvm-project/blob/main/libc/src/__support/CPP/expected.h) "
"(along with `ErrorOr<T>`, which aliases `cpp::expected<T, int>`) and "
"[`cpp::optional`](https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/blob/main/libc/src/"
"__support/CPP/optional.h) cannot throw on invalid access. Instead, their "
"observers provide two distinct safety contracts:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:181
msgid ""
"**`.value()` and `.error()` are checked in all build modes:** Calling "
"`.value()` when `!has_value()` (or `.error()` on `cpp::expected` when "
"`has_value()`) unconditionally executes `__builtin_trap()`, including in "
"release (`NDEBUG`) builds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:185
msgid ""
"**`operator*` and `operator->` are unchecked in release builds:** They trap "
"under `#ifndef NDEBUG` when `!has_value()` to catch bugs during testing, and "
"compile to direct member access with no check in release builds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:189
msgid "Choose between them as follows:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:191
msgid ""
"**Prefer `*` and `->` after an explicit check:** Once `has_value()` or "
"`operator bool` has been checked, prefer `*res` and `res->member`, "
"especially in performance-sensitive paths (such as math or formatting fast "
"paths) and whenever accessing members of the contained object (`res->field` "
"rather than `res.value().field`). When the unwrapped value is needed more "
"than once in a scope, bind it to a local variable instead of unwrapping "
"repeatedly:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:207
msgid ""
"**Use `.value()` when relying on an invariant without a branch:** If an "
"algorithm or API invariant guarantees that a value is present and you "
"intentionally omit an `if (!res)` check, you **must** use `.value()` rather "
"than `*res`. That ensures any unexpected violation traps deterministically "
"in release builds instead of reading an inactive union member."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:213
msgid ""
"**`.value()` is also acceptable for readability on `bool` or pointer "
"types:** After a dominating `if (!res)` check, writing `if (!res.value())` "
"on `ErrorOr<bool>` or `FILE *stream = res.value();` on `ErrorOr<FILE *>` "
"avoids visually ambiguous `!*res` expressions or confusion between "
"unwrapping the container and dereferencing the pointer. Under optimization, "
"jump-threading eliminates the redundant check in `res.value()` when no "
"opaque calls intervene."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:221
msgid "Assertions in libc runtime code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:223
msgid ""
"The libc developers should, and are encouraged to, use assertions freely in "
"the libc runtime code. However, the assertion should be listed via the macro "
"`LIBC_ASSERT` defined in `src/__support/libc_assert.h`. This macro can be "
"used from anywhere in the libc runtime code. Internally, all it does is to "
"print the assertion expression and exit. It does not implement the semantics "
"of the standard `assert` macro. Hence, it can be used from any where in the "
"libc runtime code without causing any recursive calls or chicken-and-egg "
"situations."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:232
msgid "Allocations in the libc runtime code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:234
msgid ""
"Some libc functions allocate memory. For example, the `strdup` function "
"allocates new memory into which the input string is duplicated. Allocations "
"are typically done by calling a function from the `malloc` family of "
"functions. Such functions can fail and return an error value to indicate "
"allocation failure. To conform to standards, the libc should handle "
"allocation failures gracefully and surface the error conditions to the user "
"code as appropriate. Since LLVM's libc is implemented in C++, we want "
"allocations and deallocations to employ C++ operators `new` and `delete` as "
"they implicitly invoke constructors and destructors respectively. However, "
"if we use the default `new` and `delete` operators, the libc will end up "
"depending on the C++ runtime. To avoid such a dependence, and to handle "
"allocation failures gracefully, we use special `new` and `delete` operators "
"defined in [`src/__support/CPP/new.h`](https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/"
"blob/main/libc/src/__support/CPP/new.h). Allocations and deallocations using "
"these operators employ a pattern like this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:266
msgid ""
"The only exception to using the above pattern is if allocating using the "
"`realloc` function is of value. In such cases, prefer to use only the "
"`malloc` family of functions for allocations and deallocations. Allocation "
"failures will still need to be handled gracefully. Further, keep in mind "
"that these functions do not call the constructors and destructors of the "
"allocated/deallocated objects. So, use these functions carefully and only "
"when it is absolutely clear that constructor and destructor invocation is "
"not required."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:275
msgid "Warnings in sources"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:277
msgid ""
"We expect contributions to be free of warnings from the [minimum supported "
"compiler versions](../compiler_support.md#minimum-supported-versions) (and "
"newer)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:280
msgid "Header Inclusion Policy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:282
msgid ""
"Because llvm-libc supports [Overlay Mode](../overlay_mode.md), [Full Host "
"Build Mode](../full_host_build.md) and [Full Cross Build Mode](../"
"full_cross_build.md) care must be taken when `#include`'ing certain headers."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:288
msgid ""
"The `include/` directory contains public facing headers that users must "
"consume for fullbuild mode. As such, types defined here will have ABI "
"implications as these definitions may differ from the underlying system for "
"overlay mode and are NEVER appropriate to include in `libc/src/` without "
"preprocessor guards for `LLVM_LIBC_FULL_BUILD`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:294
msgid ""
"Consider the case where an implementation in `libc/src/` may wish to refer "
"to a `sigset_t`, what header should be included? `<signal.h>`, `<spawn.h>`, "
"`<sys/select.h>`?"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:298
msgid ""
"None of the above. Instead, code under `src/` should `#include \"hdr/types/"
"sigset_t.h\"` which contains preprocessor guards on `LLVM_LIBC_FULL_BUILD` "
"to either include the public type (fullbuild mode) or the underlying system "
"header (overlay mode)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:303
msgid ""
"Implementations in `libc/src/` should NOT be `#include`'ing using `<>` or "
"`\"include/*`, except for these \"proxy\" headers that first check for "
"`LLVM_LIBC_FULL_BUILD`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:307
msgid ""
"These \"proxy\" headers are similarly used when referring to preprocessor "
"defines. Code under `libc/src/` should `#include` a proxy header from `hdr/"
"`, which contains a guard on `LLVM_LIBC_FULL_BUILD` to either include our "
"header from `libc/include/` (fullbuild) or the corresponding underlying "
"system header (overlay)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:313
msgid "Policy on Assembly sources"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:315
msgid ""
"Coding in high level languages such as C++ provides benefits relative to low "
"level languages like Assembly, such as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:318
msgid "Improved safety"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:320
msgid "Compile time diagnostics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:322
msgid "Instrumentation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:324
msgid "Code coverage"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:325
msgid "Profile collection"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:327
msgid "Sanitization"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:329
msgid "Automatic generation of debug info"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:331
msgid ""
"While it's not impossible to have Assembly code that correctly provides all "
"of the above, we do not wish to maintain such Assembly sources in llvm-libc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:334
msgid ""
"That said, there are a few functions provided by llvm-libc that are "
"impossible to reliably implement in C++ for all compilers supported for "
"building llvm-libc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:338
msgid ""
"We do use inline or out-of-line Assembly in an intentionally minimal set of "
"places; typically places where the stack or individual register state must "
"be manipulated very carefully for correctness, or instances where a specific "
"instruction sequence does not have a corresponding compiler builtin function "
"today."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:344
msgid ""
"Contributions adding functions implemented purely in Assembly for "
"performance are not welcome."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:347
msgid ""
"Contributors should strive to stick with C++ for as long as it remains "
"reasonable to do so. Ideally, bugs should be filed against compiler vendors, "
"and links to those bug reports should appear in commit messages or comments "
"that seek to add Assembly to llvm-libc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:352
msgid ""
"Patches containing any amount of Assembly ideally should be approved by 2 "
"maintainers. llvm-libc maintainers reserve the right to reject Assembly "
"contributions that they feel could be better maintained if rewritten in C++, "
"and to revisit this policy in the future."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:357
msgid "LIBC_NAMESPACE_DECL"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:359
msgid ""
"llvm-libc provides a macro `LIBC_NAMESPACE` which contains internal "
"implementations of libc functions and globals. This macro should only be "
"used as an identifier for accessing such symbols within the namespace (like "
"`LIBC_NAMESPACE::cpp::max`). Any usage of this namespace for declaring or "
"defining internal symbols should instead use `LIBC_NAMESPACE_DECL` which "
"declares `LIBC_NAMESPACE` with hidden visibility."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:365
msgid "Example usage:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:379
msgid ""
"Having hidden visibility on the namespace ensures extern declarations in a "
"given TU have known visibility and never generate GOT indirections. The "
"attribute guarantees this independently of global compile options and build "
"systems."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:385
msgid "Static Analysis & Clang-Tidy"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:387
msgid "Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:389
msgid "LLVM libc uses layered `.clang-tidy` configuration files:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:391
msgid ""
"`libc/.clang-tidy`: baseline checks for the `libc` subtree (currently "
"focuses on identifier naming conventions)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:393
msgid ""
"`libc/src/.clang-tidy`: adds LLVM-libc-specific checks (`llvmlibc-*`) for "
"implementation code under `libc/src` and also enables `readability-"
"identifier-naming` and `llvm-header-guard`. Diagnostics from `llvmlibc-*` "
"checks are treated as errors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:398
msgid "Running the checks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:400
msgid ""
"Configure with `-DLLVM_LIBC_ENABLE_LINTING=ON` and build the `libc-lint` "
"target. CMake looks for `clang-tidy` in the LLVM tools directory first (the "
"build tree in a bootstrapping build, so the freshly built `clang-tidy` wins) "
"and then on `PATH`. The `clang-tidy` it finds must have the same major "
"version as the compiler, because `clang-tidy` carries its own clang front "
"end and a mismatch shows up as spurious diagnostics. To use a specific "
"binary, pass `-DLLVM_LIBC_CLANG_TIDY=<path>`; this also turns linting on, "
"and a version mismatch is then reported as a warning rather than an error."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:409
msgid ""
"The `.clang-tidy` files and the `llvmlibc-*` checks live in the same "
"repository and change together, so the configuration is written for the "
"`clang-tidy` built from the same revision as the libc sources, not for the "
"latest release. An older `clang-tidy` does not fail loudly on a newer "
"configuration: it ignores checks and check options it does not know, and "
"releases up to at least 21 react to an unknown top-level key by discarding "
"the file and running their default checks, exiting 0 either way. Results "
"from an older `clang-tidy` are therefore not comparable with a build at the "
"same revision. To match, add `clang-tools-extra` to a bootstrapping build "
"and point `LLVM_LIBC_CLANG_TIDY` at the `clang-tidy` it produces; `clang-"
"tidy --verify-config --config-file=libc/src/.clang-tidy` reports anything "
"your `clang-tidy` would silently skip."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:422
msgid ""
"A configuration that asks for linting but cannot run it (no `clang-tidy`, a "
"non-clang compiler, or a discovered `clang-tidy` of the wrong major version) "
"fails to configure. There is no mode in which `libc-lint` exists but does "
"nothing, so a green `libc-lint` build always means the checks ran."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:427
msgid "LLVM-libc checks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:429
msgid "restrict-system-libc-headers"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:431
msgid "Check name: `llvmlibc-restrict-system-libc-headers`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:433
msgid ""
"One of libc-project's design goals is to use kernel headers and compiler "
"provided headers to prevent code duplication on a per platform basis. This "
"presents a problem when writing implementations since system libc headers "
"are easy to include accidentally and we can't just use the `-nostdinc` flag. "
"Improperly included system headers can introduce runtime errors because the "
"C standard outlines function prototypes and behaviors but doesn't define "
"underlying implementation details such as the layout of a struct."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:441
msgid ""
"This check prevents accidental inclusion of system libc headers when writing "
"a libc implementation."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:450
msgid "implementation-in-namespace"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:452
msgid "Check name: `llvmlibc-implementation-in-namespace`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:454
msgid ""
"All LLVM-libc implementation constructs must be enclosed in the "
"`LIBC_NAMESPACE_DECL` namespace. See {ref}`code_style` for the full "
"technical rationale and macro definitions."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:458
msgid ""
"This check ensures that top-level declarations in a translation unit are "
"enclosed within the `LIBC_NAMESPACE_DECL` namespace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:482
msgid "callee-namespace"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:484
msgid "Check name: `llvmlibc-callee-namespace`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:486
msgid ""
"LLVM-libc is distinct because it is designed to maintain interoperability "
"with other libc libraries, including the one that lives on the system. This "
"feature creates some uncertainty about which library a call resolves to "
"especially when a public header with non-namespaced functions like "
"`string.h` is included."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:491
msgid ""
"This check ensures any function call resolves to a function within the "
"LIBC_NAMESPACE namespace."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:494
msgid ""
"There are exceptions for the following functions: `__errno_location` so that "
"`errno` can be set; `malloc`, `calloc`, `realloc`, `aligned_alloc`, and "
"`free` since they are always external and can be intercepted."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:520
msgid "inline-function-decl"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:522
msgid "Check name: `llvmlibc-inline-function-decl`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../dev/code_style.md:524
msgid ""
"LLVM libc uses the `LIBC_INLINE` macro to tag inline function declarations "
"in headers. This check enforces that any inline function declaration in a "
"header begins with `LIBC_INLINE` and provides a fix-it to insert the macro."
msgstr ""
